```
</details>

At Num Specs N=1 none of these specs have below 90% pass rate:
<img width="1116" height="581" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/e2106b4b-01bd-4732-bcb3-8fbc7b91251c" />

At N=3 the all pass rate is still above 93%
<img width="690" height="301" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/e5e8cf32-292d-4b27-83b5-ed0930987c01" />

And the worst performing combinations still get 80% accuracy at N=3:
<img width="1116" height="620" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/9ad32c76-6fc1-4d1f-9170-c298fbb4adae" />

At N=5 things get harder to control.  Although on average they are fine, worst case performance gets quite bad:
<img width="1116" height="576" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/9ca81e9a-4e7d-4e2d-8eae-2bced0e2803c" />

Therefore, I decided for training to stick to N=3 specs and to go ahead with sampling 1000 combinations from this.  I also ran a few hundred trials of pairwise or 3-way comparisons and made a list of ~86 conflicts (this is a number I am pulling from memory but the actual number can be obtained by checking the specs listed above where each one lists all of the other specs it conflicts with).  This is because I wanted to make specs that did things like "filter to only math" and "filter to only code" which obviously if put together would lead to only producing empty documents.  All of this testing was done on a subset of 20 documents hand selected: 10 for being the highest quality over a range of subjects and 10 for testing a broad array of quality (some boilerplate stuff, login pages, and also some higher quality general domain information).

</details>

---

<details>
<summary>Producing the GPT-OSS-120B Data</summary>

Now that I had my 40 specs with high accuracy at N=3 combinations (for the samples I tested, I did not test all combinations) I wanted to sample 1k spec combinations and use these for training.  I sampled the specs, but because these were all derived from the same specs I wanted to rephrase them so that the model when fine-tuned wouldn't just look for spurious textual cues to decide what the rules for rephrasing were.

Using GPT-OSS-120B I used [verbalized sampling](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01171) with this prompt:

```python
META_PROMPT_TEMPLATE = """\
Generate 5 responses to the input prompt. Each response should be approximately {target_words} words.
Return the responses in JSON format with the key: "responses"
(list of dicts). Each dictionary must include:
• text: the response string only (no explanation or extra
text).
• probability: the estimated probability from 0.0 to 1.0 of
this response given the input prompt (relative to the full
distribution).
Give ONLY the JSON object, with no explanations or extra text.

---

Rewrite the below rephraser spec in a way that maintains the meaning but is completely rephrased. Feel free to shuffle the order.  I encourage you to drop the labels that are bracketed at the start of each spec detail.  Some good rewrites will be less formal than these and others will match the tone.  Try to use diverse ways to convey the information rather than using the exact same words provided in the original spec.  Including newlines (\\n) and other formatting (enumerated lists, hyphenated bullet points, etc.) when rewriting the spec is encouraged but not required for all specs.

SPEC TO REPHRASE:
{prompt}"""
```

From the outputs I renormalized from the probabilities the llm wrote for itself and sampled from those completions to get 1 rephrase per spec.  Then I started running on the Stanford NLP cluster with the sc-loprio queue and a custom orchestrator that would deliver requests to any active nodes.  I request 2 H100s/H200s and spin up vllm on them.  Here is my current progress on generating the 200M output tokens for distillation:

```
[Status] specs=101/1000 met | tokens=20,954,249 | workers=1 | warcs: ready=10 downloading=0

10%|█████▎---------------------------------------------| 20,954,249/200,000,000 tokens
```

This is after about 30 hours of running on the cluster.  I may need to get something faster going with together AI which by my estimates does inference at least 3x as fast as what I have running.

</details>

---

<details>
<summary>Training Qwen3 0.6B</summary>

Nevertheless even though I only have about 10% of the data I was looking to collect I decided to still train a preliminary Qwen3-0.6B model to see if things looked directionally correct.

At around 10M tokens (5% of the planned budget) I went and made this huggingface dataset: https://huggingface.co/datasets/MichaelR207/rephraser_small_check_0211

Using the Marin InstructionDataset and SFT scripts I launched a training run to fine-tune on these 10M tokens and the results were promising so far!  I still need to share this publicly so this link might be useless for now but I will include it: https://wandb.ai/michael-nlp/marin/runs/qwen3-0.6b-rephraser-sft-875a72

I trained on a tpu v5-p8 node and used the same 20 docs I discussed previously in the spec adherence section with 5 specs from control bench run on GPT-OSS-120B as a validation set.  The 5 specs were: "basic", "nemotron", "article_extractor", "detailed01", "hand_optimized".  I may need to reflect later if this means I need to make entirely different specs/docs for a test set later for validity, but wanted to get some signal right now:

<img width="458" height="228" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/63bec020-4817-4fc3-8bee-3c41a7d1e2ec" />

<img width="1398" height="503" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/c9800c8c-2d82-47c1-b7df-4814e12e89c7" />

This was the set of hyperparams used:

<details>
<summary>Hyperparameters</summary>

```python
DATASET_ID = "MichaelR207/rephraser_small_check_0211"
QWEN3_TOKENIZER = "Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B"
MAX_SEQ_LEN = 32_768

NUM_TRAIN_EXAMPLES = 28_678
TARGET_EPOCHS = 3
TRAIN_BATCH_SIZE = 64
NUM_TRAIN_STEPS = math.ceil(TARGET_EPOCHS * NUM_TRAIN_EXAMPLES / TRAIN_BATCH_SIZE)

sft_config = SimpleSFTConfig(
    # Hardware — v5p-8 on us-central1 (4 chips × 95 GB HBM)
    resources=ResourceConfig.with_tpu("v5p-8"),

    # Training
    train_batch_size=TRAIN_BATCH_SIZE,
    num_train_steps=NUM_TRAIN_STEPS,

    # Optimizer
    learning_rate=2e-5,
    lr_schedule="cosine",
    warmup=0.03,
    decay=0.97,  # fraction of steps for cosine decay (1.0 - warmup)
    weight_decay=0.01,
    max_grad_norm=1.0,

    # Model
    tokenizer=QWEN3_TOKENIZER,
    initialize_from_hf="Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B",
    pad_tokenizer_to_match_model=True,
    max_seq_len=MAX_SEQ_LEN,

    # Checkpointing & eval
    steps_per_eval=100,
    steps_per_checkpoint=250,
    steps_per_hf_export=250,

    seed=42,

    # Gradient accumulation: microbatch=8 (2 per device × 4 chips), 8 accum steps.
    # Logits tensor per device: 2 × 32768 × 151936 × 4B ≈ 37 GB, fits in 95 GB v5p HBM.
    per_device_parallelism=2,
)
```
</details>

A checkpoint was saved at the 250 step mark.  There are 1345 steps planned total, but I wanted early signal so I pulled out the 250 step checkpoint.  Note that now we are operating on ~20% of progress on ~10% of the planned data, so **~2% of the total data.**

</details>

---

<details>
<summary>Preliminary Results</summary>

Things directionally seem quite good!
<img width="1166" height="211" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/aba0ffef-8140-42e7-95b4-509e5f2ff859" />

The main mistakes I am seeing from the trained model are dropping and duplicating paragraphs, exactly what I saw before with Llama 3B.

<img width="1166" height="387" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/c3417f1a-26eb-4ce3-a34b-fbf5df59dbd5" />

Unfortunately these view links won't be meaningful without my codebase running, but I will include it for those meeting with me as I present these results: [view](http://localhost:8765/?config=qwen3-0.6b-sft-ckpt250-marin-parser_predict_max_tokens-4096_preproc-body_spec-as-input&tab=detail&doc=article02)

<img width="1164" height="219" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/7125df5e-5cdd-44f9-b00e-e59dd28fa631" /> [view](http://localhost:8765/?config=qwen3-0.6b-sft-ckpt250-marin-parser_predict_max_tokens-4096_preproc-body_spec-as-input&tab=detail&doc=article03)

<img width="1162" height="327" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/20f9c639-7814-4bcf-a039-6dfcd89b163d" /> [view](http://localhost:8765/?config=qwen3-0.6b-sft-ckpt250-marin-parser_predict_max_tokens-4096_preproc-body_spec-as-input&tab=detail&doc=article06)

<img width="1164" height="217" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/c4bf9978-c6a3-4c90-8ef8-a4218a4ea4d8" /> [view](http://localhost:8765/?config=qwen3-0.6b-sft-ckpt250-marin-parser_predict_max_tokens-4096_preproc-body_spec-as-input&tab=detail&doc=article04)

<img width="1162" height="652" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/3cf32b5b-fe18-4b7a-a8ef-ab951e985793" /> [view](http://localhost:8765/?config=qwen3-0.6b-sft-ckpt250-marin-parser_predict_max_tokens-4096_preproc-body_spec-as-input&spec=question_rtw&tab=detail&doc=article04)

Overall I think we are making progress in the right direction but I am excited to see if training the model for longer helps.

</details>

---

<details>
<summary>Aside: Potential bug in marin SFT for reasoning (or gap in my understanding)</summary>

The model is outputting <|start_think|><|end_think|> instead of <think></think>.  This wouldn't be so bad but I checked and these aren't special tokens in my tokenizer which I think I setup properly with marin:

```
<|start_think|> -> [27, 91, 2468, 5854, 766, 91, 29] -> ['<', '|', 'start', '_th', 'ink', '|', '>']
<|end_think|> -> [27, 91, 408, 5854, 766, 91, 29] -> ['<', '|', 'end', '_th', 'ink', '|', '>']
```

This seems strictly worse than the special tokens, but seems like a design decision here: https://github.com/marin-community/marin/issues/905

Maybe I am making a mistake in how I use it.

</details>

---

Next Steps:

- Test at later checkpoints
- Continue producing the data
- Plan interesting experiments now that we can rephrase

topics.body:
Build each shootout arm's prebuilt Levanter rows from canonical generation traces:
- plain: one clean passing trajectory per eligible task, either the first or the one with the fewest reasoning tokens;
- RS-multi: per epoch, one clean passing attempt per plain task slot, drawn without replacement;
- relay: passing relay episodes with a GLM turn, with each family held to its own share of plain's B;
- recovery: plain's rows, with masked failed attempts and the harness's replies inserted before teacher turns in 20% of rows. A TMax failure is a seeded variant of one of v0's parser-error styles, labeled by the error the vendored Terminus-2 parser gives it;
- validation: one shared set, the first clean passing trajectory per held-out task.

The loss covers GLM's turns only. v0's turns in relay episodes stay as context.

A trajectory is dropped when:
- it was summarized, failed or raised;
- it did not end on the agent's own completion (`--require-clean-completion`: mini-swe-agent's submit, or Terminus-2's `"task_complete": true`);
- it does not fit 32,768 tokens;
- it is listed in `--exclude-trials`, a JSON-lines file of attempts whose pass cannot be trusted (`trial_name`, optional `retry` and `task_id`), applied before selection;
- it leaks: a live proxy capability token (decoded JWT, audience iris-proxy), a read of mini-swe-agent's own tool environment, or a read of a library copy of the task's repo.

Every arm gets the same replay rows: a seeded prefix of the replay slice, cut at R = round(B / 4) and checked by hash against plain's manifest.

Each arm writes three epoch files. Each is its own seeded permutation, padded with zero-loss rows to a multiple of 16, so a trainer that reads files in order with mixture block 16 sees every row exactly once per epoch. The `.gz` files have a fixed gzip header (mtime 0, no file name), so their hashes depend only on their rows.

The manifest records:
- per-epoch row counts, steps and `rows_sha256`;
- per-family supervised and context tokens;
- the builder commit and source hashes;
- `tokenizer_source` (v0 @ 4eeb756b, whose tokenizer files v0.1 shares byte for byte), `parent_model` (v0.1 or v0, via `--parent`) and, on the relay arm, `relay_student`;
- each input trace file's sha256 and Harbor commit, and the excluded trials;
- the token gate over the exact inputs;
- the pool's held-out ids.

A production prefix gets `_SUCCESS` last. `--dry-run` uses the same pins and checks, but writes a `DRY_RUN_NOT_FOR_TRAINING` marker and no `_SUCCESS`.

`chain.py` builds one round's arms from one trace prefix in one command: validation, plain, RS-multi, relay, then recovery, all with the same pool, replay and clean rule. `--relay-trace-prefix` reads relay rounds from a different prefix than plain. Each relay round's `driver_config.json` must name the `--relay-student`. If a relay family ends below plain's B for it, the chain also builds `plain-matched-relay/`, which is plain cut to relay's total in that family, and says so in its summary. `verify_arms.py` then re-reads plain, RS-multi, relay and recovery together, and the matched plain on its own. It checks that no held-out rows are present, that replay rows are shared, that each epoch is a permutation of the same rows, the per-family budgets, and recovery against plain row by row. The chain prints one `CHAIN_BUILT` summary. `verify_arms.py` holds RS-multi to 1.5% of B per epoch and 1% on the mean of its 3 epochs: the sampler draws one random clean pass per task per epoch and does not aim at B, and the v7 HIGH arm lands at −1.10%, −0.77% and +0.86% (mean −0.34%).

A production chain first runs `pool_gate.py` and builds nothing unless it passes. For plain, every shard in the round's shard lists must have `_SUCCESS` and `rebuilt.jsonl`. For relay, each family's `STOP` marker must exist, no round may be uploaded without `_SUCCESS`, and every round with `_SUCCESS` must have its `rebuilt.jsonl`. Every finished shard's traces and requests are then re-scanned for capability tokens, and any hit fails the gate. A running round is invisible to the gate, because a round's directory is uploaded only when it finishes. So all generation jobs must have exited before the build.

`progress.py` and `progress_table.py` report a partial round's arm sizes while generation runs. `reasoning_trim.py` makes dry variants of a built plain arm with long reasoning masked or dropped.

On the round-7 plain traces (GLM at reasoning effort high, pool v5), a dry build at this head gives:
- plain B = 19,418,246 supervised tokens: 7,543,248 SWE-smith and 11,874,998 TMax;
- each epoch is 3,759 rows, or 235 steps at batch 16;
- RS-multi is within 0.76% (SWE) and 1.33% (TMax) of plain's per-family totals;
- recovery injects into 263 of 1,317 SWE rows and 295 of 1,531 TMax rows.

Two builds at the same commit give identical `rows_sha256` and identical `.gz` hashes.

